Mult.catalogue_injective, ZInQ.Mult.Hcat_squarefree, ZInQ.Mult.padicValNat_ell, ZInQ.Mult.not_mem_S_mul","summary":"1. $M_0\\ge1$, so $c_*=4M_0\\ge4$, and $\\ell_0\\ge1$. 2. $S_{\\rm mul}$ is a finite set of primes containing every prime $p\\le324$; every $p\\in S_{\\rm mul}$ satisfies $p\\le R$; and $R\\ge D$, $R\\ge10^{12}$. Hence the six-contact estimate applies with $S=S_{\\rm mul}$ and this $R$, and its constant is $H_6(S_{\\rm mul};R)=A_{\\rm ht}$ (as real numbers, $Q=R^{RE_0}$ being an integer power). Under the minimal height…","route":"../roadmap/multiplication/certificate/index.html#constants-properties"},{"id":"multiplication/certificate/local-templates","title":"Semantics of the local-parameter, inverse-abscissa and logarithm templates","status":"fully_proved","caption":"Theorem","lean":"ZInQ.Mult.rawZ_rel_iff_of, ZInQ.Mult.rawZ_not_rel, ZInQ.Mult.rawInverseX_rel_iff_of, ZInQ.Mult.rawInverseX_not_rel, ZInQ.Mult.log25_rel_iff, ZInQ.Mult.rawZ_rel_iota, ZInQ.Mult.rawInverseX_rel_iota","summary":"Let $Q\\in E(K_0)$ with code $p=\\operatorname{code}(Q)$, and let $\\zeta,v,\\lambda$ be rational pairs, $[u]=u_0+u_1s$.","route":"../roadmap/multiplication/certificate/index.html#local-templates"},{"id":"multiplication/certificate/twelve-tests","title":"The twelve local tests","status":"fully_proved","caption":"Definition","lean":"ZInQ.Mult.invX, ZInQ.Mult.zOf, ZInQ.Mult.Lam, ZInQ.Mult.testValues, ZInQ.Mult.testValues_eq","summary":"Let $h,z_1,z_2,z_3,c_0,c_1,c_2,c_3\\in\\mathbb Z$ with $h\\ne0$ and $h\\ne c_0$. Put so that $V_0=H-[c_0]P_0$ and $V_i=U_i-[c_i]P_0$. For $Q\\in\\mathbb ZP_0$ let $\\rho(Q)=1/x(Q)$ with $\\rho(O)=0$, and $\\Lambda(Q)=L(z(Q))$ with $\\Lambda(O)=L(0)=0$, where $L$ is the truncated logarithm. Put With $\\sigma$ the conjugation of $K_0$, the twelve test values $\\tau(h,z;c)=(\\tau_1,\\dots,\\tau_{12})\\in K_0^{12}$ are…","route":"../roadmap/multiplication/certificate/index.html#twelve-tests"},{"id":"multiplication/certificate/test-group-semantics","title":"Semantics of the test-group template","status":"fully_proved","caption":"Theorem","lean":"ZInQ.Mult.testGroup_rel_iff, ZInQ.Mult.testGroup_rel_iff_codes","summary":"For all $c_0,c_1,c_2,c_3\\in\\mathbb Z$ and all rational pairs $D_1,\\dots,D_{12}$, \\iff\\exists\\,h,z_1,z_2,z_3\\in\\mathbb Z:\\ h\\ne0,\\ h\\ne c_0,\\ [D_j]=\\tau_j(h,z;c)\\ (1\\le j\\le12).$$ (The template's output pairs $D_0,\\dots,D_{11}$ are $D_1,\\dots,D_{12}$ here.) The four inputs must be canonical codes, as they are in every use: the raw addition contract is only valid on canonical codes.","route":"../roadmap/multiplication/certificate/index.html#test-group-semantics"},{"id":"multiplication/certificate/slope-predicate","title":"The slope predicate","status":"fully_proved","caption":"Definition","lean":"ZInQ.Mult.SlopePred, ZInQ.Mult.slopeR0, ZInQ.Mult.slopeA, ZInQ.Mult.slopeR, ZInQ.Mult.normTests, ZInQ.Mult.groupW, ZInQ.Mult.traceTheta, ZInQ.Mult.certShifts","summary":"For $t,\\ell\\in\\mathbb Q$ and $c=(c_0,c_1,c_2,c_3)\\in\\mathbb Z^4$, the slope predicate $\\mathrm{Slope}(t,\\ell;c)$ holds when all of the following hold:","route":"../roadmap/multiplication/certificate/index.html#slope-predicate"},{"id":"multiplication/certificate/slope-semantics","title":"Semantics of the slope template","status":"fully_proved","caption":"Theorem","lean":"ZInQ.Mult.slope_rel_iff","summary":"For all $t,\\ell\\in\\mathbb Q$ and $c=(c_0,c_1,c_2,c_3)\\in\\mathbb Z^4$,","route":"../roadmap/multiplication/certificate/index.html#slope-semantics"},{"id":"multiplication/certificate/height-certificate-semantics","title":"Semantics of the height certificate template","status":"fully_proved","caption":"Theorem","lean":"ZInQ.Mult.heightCertificate_rel_iff, ZInQ.Mult.HeightCert","summary":"For all $Y_1,Y_2,Y_3\\in\\mathbb Q^5$, $\\ell\\in\\mathbb Q$ and $c\\in\\mathbb Z^4$, the relation $\\mathrm{Rel}_{\\mathtt{height\\_certificate}}(Y_1,Y_2,Y_3,\\ell,\\iota(c_0),\\iota(c_1),\\iota(c_2),\\iota(c_3))$ hold